# Read by ripgrep in addition to VCS ignore files, and — unlike them — still
# honoured under `--no-ignore-vcs`, which ripgreprc turns on globally. That
# split is the point: VCS ignore hides whole nested repos from a search rooted
# above them, while these are paths that should stay out of every search.
# ripgreprc holds flags, this file holds paths, and neither does the other's
# job.
#
# Generic entries only. Anything true of one workspace or repo belongs in its
# own `.ignore`, which ripgrep reads from ancestors of the search path.

# Noise: directories nobody ever wants to read.
node_modules/

# Worktree checkouts are excluded per repo instead, in that repo's own .ignore:
# a multi-component pattern here does not match from above a repo root, while a
# basename one broad enough to work would hide directories nobody meant to hide.
.venv/
__pycache__/
dist/
build/
.next/
.nuxt/
.turbo/
.svelte-kit/
coverage/
.vercel/
*.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json

# Secrets, which are here for a different reason than noise. Searching a file
# READS it: `rg SOME_KEY` lands on the line that assigns SOME_KEY, and the
# value is in the transcript before anyone chose to open anything. "Don't read
# files that may hold secrets" cannot cover that, because nobody was reading —
# so ignoring them is what makes that rule hold, and these entries are a safety
# mechanism rather than a convenience. `--no-ignore` still reaches the real file
# when the real file is the point; use `-l` when it does, or the value lands in
# the transcript anyway.
#
# Narrow on purpose. `.mise.toml` is tracked, shared, holds no secrets, and is
# the first thing anyone greps to learn what a repo configures, so the tempting
# `*mise*.toml` is wrong — only the `local` forms hold values.
.env
.env.*
.mise*.local.toml
mise*.local.toml
*secrets*.env
*credentials.json
*.pem
*.key

# Ignoring a file costs discovery: `rg SOME_KEY` no longer finds where SOME_KEY
# is declared, which is how anyone learns it exists at all. A tracked
# `<filename>.example` beside each ignored file pays that back, carrying the
# names without the values — `agents/AGENTS.md`, "Secrets & sensitive files",
# has the requirement and what such a file may and may not contain. This line
# is last in the file because a later pattern overrides an earlier one.
!*.example
